WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.167
-Jimmy Buffett, we love you.

00:00:02.167 --> 00:00:03.501
Thank you so much for coming on.
-Jimmy Fallon.

00:00:03.501 --> 00:00:04.868
-I know,
Jimmy and Jimmy right here.

00:00:04.868 --> 00:00:06.400
-Right, yeah.
-Thank you for singing

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:08.133
"Margaritaville"
earlier in the show.

00:00:08.133 --> 00:00:10.234
-Let's talk about
Florence Welch, huh?

00:00:10.234 --> 00:00:11.734
-I mean,
can we just talk about that?

00:00:11.734 --> 00:00:13.434
-Doing "Margaritaville."
Come on, please.

00:00:13.434 --> 00:00:14.734
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:14.734 --> 00:00:17.434
-She's got everything, right?
She's got the voice.

00:00:17.434 --> 00:00:20.367
-I know.
That was, like, angelic kind of.

00:00:20.367 --> 00:00:23.901
-Yeah, take that on tour.
You were saying backstage, I go,

00:00:23.901 --> 00:00:25.000
"Thanks for doing the bit,"
and you go,

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.133
"Yeah, well,
I sang 'Margaritaville'

00:00:27.133 --> 00:00:29.200
for the first time on
"The Tonight Show."

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:30.834
-That's right.
-With Johnny Carson.

00:00:30.834 --> 00:00:33.200
-Yeah, 41 years ago.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:36.868
41 years?
Oh, oh!

00:00:36.868 --> 00:00:38.801
-I have a photo of it.
-Oh, my God.

00:00:38.801 --> 00:00:41.133
-Here's a picture of you
and Johnny, right there.

00:00:41.133 --> 00:00:43.033
[ Applause ]

00:00:43.033 --> 00:00:45.601
-Oh, that mustache.
No wonder it went.

00:00:45.601 --> 00:00:47.567
-Look at that mustache.
I think you look like a stud.

00:00:47.567 --> 00:00:48.734
Come on, bud.

00:00:48.734 --> 00:00:50.000
-No, that's a little
porn-looking mustache.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:51.367
[ Laughter ]
-Oh, no.

00:00:51.367 --> 00:00:54.767
-No, I never -- no.
-41 years ago.

00:00:54.767 --> 00:00:57.367
This song has stood
the test of time.

00:00:57.367 --> 00:00:58.701
-It has.
-Do you remember

00:00:58.701 --> 00:01:01.667
going on "Carson"?
-Oh, yes. Yes.

00:01:01.667 --> 00:01:03.734
-What was it --
-It was a transitional time

00:01:03.734 --> 00:01:06.033
between, like --
Most of the acts before,

00:01:06.033 --> 00:01:09.100
there were Vegas acts
and people Johnny knew or liked.

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:11.100
And I guess the network probably

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:12.801
was pushing
rock and roll in there.

00:01:12.801 --> 00:01:16.634
So I studied it for like two
weeks like it was a course,

00:01:16.634 --> 00:01:18.033
'cause I wanted
to know every move,

00:01:18.033 --> 00:01:22.167
and I wanted to know who was
good, who could talk, and who --

00:01:22.167 --> 00:01:24.067
Because he was a straight man.
You know that.

00:01:24.067 --> 00:01:26.868
-Of course, yeah, exactly.
Johnny would set you up, and...

00:01:26.868 --> 00:01:29.367
-And so
they had Jimi Hendrix on,

00:01:29.367 --> 00:01:33.467
and Jimi Hendrix had bad trouble
with whatever the sound was,

00:01:33.467 --> 00:01:36.033
and he stopped
in the middle of the song.

00:01:36.033 --> 00:01:37.400
And then started again.

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:40.033
So it was like
a transitional point.

00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:44.501
When I got on, they were not
very kind of friendly to me.

00:01:44.501 --> 00:01:46.868
They said, "Listen,
the one thing you can't do

00:01:46.868 --> 00:01:50.434
is stop because Johnny
doesn't like it when you stop."

00:01:50.434 --> 00:01:52.734
And so I'd studied, I said,
"Well, I'm gonna do

00:01:52.734 --> 00:01:55.334
'Margaritaville,'"
and then he said, you know,

00:01:55.334 --> 00:01:58.434
"You'll never go to the desk.
Maybe he does it sometimes.

00:01:58.434 --> 00:02:02.133
But in case he waves you,
then you walk over.

00:02:02.133 --> 00:02:04.200
But it's not gonna happen."
[ Laughter ]

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:05.968
-"Anyway, thanks for being
here," yeah, yeah.

00:02:05.968 --> 00:02:07.501
-Yeah, "Get out of here."
-Yeah, "Get out of here."

00:02:07.501 --> 00:02:09.767
Good luck." Yeah.
-So I said, "Okay."

00:02:09.767 --> 00:02:11.868
I was just happy to be on
"The Tonight Show."

00:02:11.868 --> 00:02:13.801
I mean, come on.
I'd studied it.

00:02:13.801 --> 00:02:16.367
So what I forgot about was,
the good thing was,

00:02:16.367 --> 00:02:19.200
there were Parrot Heads
in the audience.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:20.334
-Aha.
-Got any out here now?

00:02:20.334 --> 00:02:21.501
-Yeah!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:21.501 --> 00:02:22.934
-Oh, thank God,
they're still here!

00:02:22.934 --> 00:02:25.334
-Of course, they're everywhere.

00:02:25.334 --> 00:02:27.634
-But they started yelling.
And Johnny kind of caught on

00:02:27.634 --> 00:02:29.801
to the fact that there was
a crowd there.

00:02:29.801 --> 00:02:32.901
And so, me, I didn't know
that much about television,

00:02:32.901 --> 00:02:35.133
so I'm playing to the audience
and never look into the camera.

00:02:35.133 --> 00:02:38.300
That's the first one.
So -- But when we started

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:41.534
the song, we were off tempo

00:02:41.534 --> 00:02:43.501
because the crowd
was yelling so much.

00:02:43.501 --> 00:02:46.234
So I basically [bleep] up
"Margaritaville"

00:02:46.234 --> 00:02:47.501
on the "Johnny Carson Show."
[ Laughter ]

00:02:47.501 --> 00:02:51.434
-Oh, my gosh!
But did you -- did you stop?

00:02:51.434 --> 00:02:53.300
-No.
-That's exactly right!

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:55.467
That's the lesson learned --
never stop.

00:02:55.467 --> 00:02:59.567
-No, as the band knows,
there's an old credo is,

00:02:59.567 --> 00:03:02.834
"When you get lost,
meet me on one," right?

00:03:02.834 --> 00:03:05.601
-Yeah.
-So we got on one, we did it,

00:03:05.601 --> 00:03:07.167
and then I [bleep] up --
I'm going,

00:03:07.167 --> 00:03:08.467
"This is never gonna happen."

00:03:08.467 --> 00:03:11.067
I looked, and he was going --
-He waved you over.

00:03:11.067 --> 00:03:14.400
-And I went over, and I was
lucky enough to do that show.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:15.534
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:15.534 --> 00:03:18.801
-How many times?
-I think 15 or 16 times.

00:03:18.801 --> 00:03:21.734
Then he actually invited me
on the last week.

00:03:21.734 --> 00:03:23.801
What a great guy.
And just a pro.

00:03:23.801 --> 00:03:25.734
-Became friends with him.
-Yeah, but I'm --

00:03:25.734 --> 00:03:27.000
That's so weird.
I'm getting goose bumps.

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:28.701
41 years later, I'm still on
"The Tonight Show."

00:03:28.701 --> 00:03:31.033
-Yeah, but come on.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:31.033 --> 00:03:33.267
Now you get an intro
with the "one and only."

00:03:33.267 --> 00:03:36.234
Now you're the one and only
Jimmy Buffett.

00:03:36.234 --> 00:03:39.834
You know, I love you so much
because you're just a performer,

00:03:39.834 --> 00:03:43.334
and you're an artist,
and you need the audience.

00:03:43.334 --> 00:03:45.734
-Yeah.
-And when the pandemic hit,

00:03:45.734 --> 00:03:47.667
I really thought of you --
you're one of the first people

00:03:47.667 --> 00:03:50.534
I thought of, because I go,
"He must be going insane."

00:03:50.534 --> 00:03:52.601
'Cause you love to perform.
You don't stop.

00:03:52.601 --> 00:03:55.801
-Absolutely.
Above anything

00:03:55.801 --> 00:03:56.901
that I've been lucky enough
to do,

00:03:56.901 --> 00:03:58.467
performing is what I love to do.

00:03:58.467 --> 00:04:00.868
But I also, as a sailor,
I have been on

00:04:00.868 --> 00:04:04.267
some rough voyages, and you just
have to keep going that way.

00:04:04.267 --> 00:04:07.834
And so I just kind of --
you know, I had to take that

00:04:07.834 --> 00:04:10.701
kind of sailor mentality
to steer through

00:04:10.701 --> 00:04:12.501
the bad currents
that were going on.

00:04:12.501 --> 00:04:15.868
So what we were lucky enough to
do, because we had already had

00:04:15.868 --> 00:04:18.667
Radio Margaritaville,
we had Margaritaville TV,

00:04:18.667 --> 00:04:23.234
and my daughter, who was doing
filmmaking at the time,

00:04:23.234 --> 00:04:25.400
got me saying,
"Well, you got to just

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:28.200
play to people
on both of those things."

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:29.934
So we stayed in touch
with our audience

00:04:29.934 --> 00:04:32.434
on Radio Margaritaville,
Margaritaville TV.

00:04:32.434 --> 00:04:37.334
People that were still working
and first responders

00:04:37.334 --> 00:04:39.868
and people in hospitals,
and people got on.

00:04:39.868 --> 00:04:41.434
At least we were
staying in touch,

00:04:41.434 --> 00:04:43.434
but we weren't
physically in touch.

00:04:43.434 --> 00:04:48.267
So physically in touch came --
the first show was 10 tables,

00:04:48.267 --> 00:04:51.267
four people to a table,
at a place called Belly Up

00:04:51.267 --> 00:04:53.934
down in Southern California.

00:04:53.934 --> 00:04:55.667
-Now you really made it.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:55.667 --> 00:04:57.901
-I said, "Am I back
to the beginning?"

00:04:57.901 --> 00:05:00.300
-Belly Up --
that's a great name.

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:02.067
-Was I time traveling
in the other direction?

00:05:02.067 --> 00:05:05.334
-40 people, and you go, "Wow."
-And so what it was --

00:05:05.334 --> 00:05:09.267
And we went to rehearsal before
that so three of us were in --

00:05:09.267 --> 00:05:11.868
in California, and two of us
were in New York,

00:05:11.868 --> 00:05:14.901
and they Zoomed in.
And the three of us

00:05:14.901 --> 00:05:18.567
in California were in my piano
player Mike Utley's apartment,

00:05:18.567 --> 00:05:21.267
and we started to play,
and we broke down crying,

00:05:21.267 --> 00:05:22.667
it was that emotional.

00:05:22.667 --> 00:05:24.767
We hadn't seen each other --
because that's the other family.

00:05:24.767 --> 00:05:27.467
-Exactly.
-And so at least we did it then

00:05:27.467 --> 00:05:29.834
because when we got in front
of the 40 people,

00:05:29.834 --> 00:05:31.367
they started crying immediately.

00:05:31.367 --> 00:05:32.567
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah!

00:05:32.567 --> 00:05:35.868
"Because we missed it."
-They missed it so much.

00:05:35.868 --> 00:05:39.100
So we went from 40 people
initially and tried to

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:42.801
work our way up safely,
and then last --

00:05:42.801 --> 00:05:45.534
Sunday night, we played to
60,000 people in New Orleans.

00:05:45.534 --> 00:05:47.267
-That's what I'm talking about.
Come on.

00:05:47.267 --> 00:05:48.567
We're coming back.
-Thank you.

00:05:48.567 --> 00:05:50.801
-We're coming back.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:50.801 --> 00:05:53.067
New Orleans.
-In New Orleans, yeah.

00:05:53.067 --> 00:05:55.767
-Now, that's a place
right there, New Orleans.

00:05:55.767 --> 00:06:00.334
When you play there, I mean, if
you crush there, you've made it.

00:06:00.334 --> 00:06:01.868
-Yeah.
-Because that's the good crowd.

00:06:01.868 --> 00:06:03.467
That's a good spirit
right there.

00:06:03.467 --> 00:06:06.434
-I started out on Bourbon Street
as a folk singer.

00:06:06.434 --> 00:06:07.567
-You did?
-Yes.

00:06:07.567 --> 00:06:10.000
-Wow.
What was that?

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:13.200
-Well, I was working, and --
There was a lot more music

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:15.467
on Bourbon Street then
than there was

00:06:15.467 --> 00:06:18.534
the latest attractions.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:18.534 --> 00:06:21.200
And so all the musicians
that I really loved,

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:23.701
jazz and other people,
were there.

00:06:23.701 --> 00:06:26.400
And I worked in a little place
called The Bayou Room

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:30.501
for kind of a lower-level
Mafia pair of brothers.

00:06:30.501 --> 00:06:31.801
And, uh --
[ Laughter ]

00:06:31.801 --> 00:06:34.167
And so I was just so happy
to be there,

00:06:34.167 --> 00:06:37.667
and I made $150 a week
and half price on drinks.

00:06:37.667 --> 00:06:39.701
And the first week,
I owed them money.

00:06:39.701 --> 00:06:41.601
[ Laughter ]

00:06:41.601 --> 00:06:44.133
-That's legendary.
Come on, that is legendary.

00:06:44.133 --> 00:06:46.133
-I got into it.
Listen, as an ex-Catholic

00:06:46.133 --> 00:06:49.133
altar boy, I went hard.
-Yeah, right, yeah, yeah.

00:06:49.133 --> 00:06:52.133
-But I did start there.
And that was really

00:06:52.133 --> 00:06:55.467
my musical roots
came in New Orleans.

00:06:55.467 --> 00:06:58.367
And I've stayed connected
to that

00:06:58.367 --> 00:07:00.334
for as long as I've been here.

00:07:00.334 --> 00:07:01.701
-let's talk about your new
documentary,

00:07:01.701 --> 00:07:04.133
"Jazzfest: A New Orleans Story."

00:07:04.133 --> 00:07:07.334
I mean, Jazzfest is legendary,
but I don't know if anyone knows

00:07:07.334 --> 00:07:09.534
the history
and the story behind it.

00:07:09.534 --> 00:07:12.334
-You know, to me, I think,
Jimmy, what starts it,

00:07:12.334 --> 00:07:16.467
Jazzfest is like the child of
Mardi Gras, you know?

00:07:16.467 --> 00:07:17.801
'Cause I grew up
as a child of Mardi Gras,

00:07:17.801 --> 00:07:19.133
because you were allowed --

00:07:19.133 --> 00:07:22.300
You know,
fun is important in this world.

00:07:22.300 --> 00:07:25.167
-Thank you.
-As we're learning now,

00:07:25.167 --> 00:07:27.100
when you see audiences
coming back,

00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:30.067
they're so amped up to just see
a performance.

00:07:30.067 --> 00:07:32.334
-It's the greatest.
There's nothing like it.

00:07:32.334 --> 00:07:33.701
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:33.701 --> 00:07:36.067
It's tough for us
not to get emotional.

00:07:36.067 --> 00:07:40.100
-And so Jazzfest, I think I went
to the third or fourth one.

00:07:40.100 --> 00:07:42.734
And our other friend,
Quint Davis, who helped,

00:07:42.734 --> 00:07:46.033
who was instrumental in finding
the right musicians

00:07:46.033 --> 00:07:49.767
for the documentary
with Frank Marshall,

00:07:49.767 --> 00:07:53.601
he ran a little head shop called
The Love Shop, and we met then,

00:07:53.601 --> 00:07:56.234
when I was going in
to buy papers.

00:07:56.234 --> 00:07:58.367
And so --
-Not newspapers.

00:07:58.367 --> 00:08:00.834
-No, not newspapers.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:00.834 --> 00:08:04.267
So I experienced my whole
kind of musical beginnings

00:08:04.267 --> 00:08:06.501
on Bourbon Street
and in New Orleans,

00:08:06.501 --> 00:08:09.400
and so I went to the festival
that early and continued

00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:12.100
just to go until they asked me
to come play it,

00:08:12.100 --> 00:08:13.801
and that was like 20 years ago.

00:08:13.801 --> 00:08:15.501
I played it
pretty much ever since.

00:08:15.501 --> 00:08:18.534
-It's pretty fun
It's actually beautiful.

00:08:18.534 --> 00:08:20.200
I want to show everyone a clip.
Here's Jimmy Buffett

00:08:20.200 --> 00:08:21.901
in "Jazzfest:
A New Orleans Story."

00:08:21.901 --> 00:08:23.000
Take a look at this.

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:25.300
-I grew up on the Gulf Coast.

00:08:25.300 --> 00:08:28.033
I was an 18-year-old
virgin Jesuit altar boy,

00:08:28.033 --> 00:08:30.667
and I came to New Orleans
to not be that.

00:08:30.667 --> 00:08:35.033
♪ I come here each season ♪

00:08:35.033 --> 00:08:37.601
We couldn't get to California,
so we kind of hippied out

00:08:37.601 --> 00:08:39.067
right here in New Orleans.

00:08:39.067 --> 00:08:42.601
♪♪

00:08:42.601 --> 00:08:46.067
♪ But it's a real beauty ♪

00:08:46.067 --> 00:08:50.200
♪ Oh, these Louisiana cuties ♪

00:08:50.200 --> 00:08:53.167
♪ And I know I got cutie that
slid over from Mississippi ♪

00:08:53.167 --> 00:08:55.267
♪ And got a clue ♪

00:08:55.267 --> 00:08:58.133
Everything about the songs,
I heard the melodies,

00:08:58.133 --> 00:09:00.534
and the rhythms that I loved
came out of here.

00:09:00.534 --> 00:09:02.667
♪ Again in Margaritaville ♪

00:09:02.667 --> 00:09:04.200
-Oh, my goodness.

00:09:04.200 --> 00:09:06.567
Jimmy Buffett, everybody.
-Thank you.

00:09:06.567 --> 00:09:07.801
-Always good to see you.

00:09:07.801 --> 00:09:10.968
Go see "Jazzfest:
A New Orleans Story."

00:09:10.968 --> 00:09:13.467
Florence + The Machine perform
after the break.

00:09:13.467 --> 00:09:15.801
Stick around, everybody.
Jimmy Buffett!

